diff --git a/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
index 45f6c7f..cb98c0a 100644
--- a/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
+++ b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
@@ -65,6 +65,8 @@ Two more are for a port that goes further than the PSP did, and both are null un
 
 `extra_buttons` names the buttons a modern pad has and a PSP lacks (`input::kExtraL2`, `kExtraR2`, `kExtraL3`, `kExtraR3`) that the port itself gives a meaning, reading them with `hle_extension::extra_buttons()`; the game never sees them. With L2 or R2 named, the triggers press those instead of copies of L and R (a trigger profile the player picks still wins), and Keyboard and mouse lists bindings for the buttons named (`input.bind.l2` and so on; 1 and 3 are L2 and R2 by default). `<prefix>_NO_EXTRA_BUTTONS=1` treats it as empty. Without it the triggers press L and R and the menu lists none of them.
 
+For a picture of the port's own beside one of the game's screens, such as a hint by a menu the port extends, `ui::add_game_overlay` (`host/ui/ui.hpp`) draws with Dear ImGui over every game frame it asks for, `ui::game_picture()` says where the game's 480x272 picture is in the window, and `ui::game_text_font()` is the face the game's own text is drawn with.
+
 `tilt` is for a game played by tilting its world, as LocoRoco is with L and R: the buttons that tilt left and right, whether the game eases its tilt towards the held side (then a small roll of the device presses the button for part of the frames and tilts the world part of the way), and a sentence for the menu. With it, Controls gets a Tilt controls section, off by default, and the motion sensors of a gamepad (DualShock 4, DualSense, Switch Pro, a Steam Deck seen through SDL's own driver) or of the phone itself press those buttons (`host/input/tilt.hpp` for the arithmetic, `host/input/motion.hpp` for the sensors). Find the buttons and the easing with the game's pad trace before filling it in. Without it, nothing opens a sensor and the menu has no such section.
 
 `player_name_label` and `default_player_name` are what the interface calls the name a game asks for and what it answers before the player sets one: "Player name" and "Player" unless a profile says otherwise. Yakumo says "Hunter name" and "Hunter".
